# Everything the image does not need, and two things it must never have.
#
# `.env` is the second. It is gitignored, so it cannot arrive through the
# repository — but a Docker build context is the working directory, not
# the git index, so an untracked `.env` sitting next to the Dockerfile
# would be copied straight into a layer. Baking a credential into an
# image is worse than committing one: the image is pushed to a registry,
# layers are cached and shared, and there is no `git rm` for a published
# layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The local virtualenv. Copying it in would shadow the one the builder
# creates, with host-compiled binaries that may not match the image's
# architecture or libc.
.venv/
venv/

.git/
.github/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Tests are deliberately excluded. They are run in CI against the source,
# not in the image against a deployment: shipping them would add the test
# fixtures — including `tests/fixtures/security/planted_credential.py`,
# which exists specifically to look like a leaked key — to a production
# artifact.
tests/

# Provider response cache and checkpoints. Local development artefacts
# that would be stale in an image and large.
.cache/

docs/
*.md
!README.md
